head:
  - tag: title
    content: "Swiss VAT methods: effective or net tax rate | nullish"
---

{% aside type="tip" title="In short" %}
Two choices decide how your VAT works. The settlement method decides how it's calculated: the effective method deducts the VAT you pay, the net tax rate applies a fixed rate to your turnover. The remuneration method decides when it's due: when you invoice, or when you're paid.
{% /aside %}

## The two choices

| Choice              | Options                   | What it decides                   |
| ------------------- | ------------------------- | --------------------------------- |
| Settlement method   | Effective or net tax rate | How the VAT you owe is calculated |
| Remuneration method | Agreed or received        | When VAT on a sale is due         |

Both apply on top of the same rates and the same invoices. Your clients don't see which ones you use.

## Effective method

You charge VAT on your sales, and deduct the VAT you paid on your purchases and investments, the input tax. You pay the difference.

It's the default, and it suits everyone. Declarations are usually quarterly.

## Net tax rate method

You pay a fixed percentage of your turnover, VAT included. The tax administration sets that rate by industry. You don't deduct input tax: it's already built into the rate ([Art. 37 VAT Act](https://www.fedlex.admin.ch/eli/cc/2009/615/en#art_37)).

It's simpler: no input tax to track, and declarations twice a year. Since 2025, according to the tax administration:

- your taxable turnover, VAT included, can't exceed CHF 5.024 million a year;
- your VAT due can't exceed CHF 108,000 a year;
- you can use more than two net tax rates, one for each activity above 10 % of your turnover.

Your invoices don't change: they still show the normal VAT rate.

## Example

A consultant invoices CHF 200,000 in a year, VAT at 8.1 % included. Suppose the tax administration grants a net tax rate of 6.2 %.

| Method       | Calculation                                 | VAT due (CHF) |
| ------------ | ------------------------------------------- | ------------- |
| Effective    | 14,986 of VAT on sales − 3,000 of input tax | 11,986        |
| Net tax rate | 200,000 × 6.2 %                             | 12,400        |

With CHF 3,000 of input tax, the effective method costs CHF 414 less. Below about CHF 2,586 of input tax, the net tax rate would be cheaper. It's a trade-off between a few hundred francs and less work.

## Agreed or received

- **Agreed**: VAT is due when you invoice, even if the client hasn't paid yet. It's the default.
- **Received**: VAT is due when the client pays. You have to request it from the tax administration ([Art. 39 VAT Act](https://www.fedlex.admin.ch/eli/cc/2009/615/en#art_39)).

Received helps when clients pay late: you don't advance VAT on money you haven't seen. Agreed keeps the declarations in line with your invoices.

## Quarterly, semi-annual or annual

| Method       | Usual period | Annual settlement, since 2025                     |
| ------------ | ------------ | ------------------------------------------------- |
| Effective    | Quarterly    | Three instalments: 30 May, 30 August, 30 November |
| Net tax rate | Semi-annual  | One instalment: 30 August                         |

Since 2025, companies with a turnover up to CHF 5,005,000 can request to settle VAT once a year. The request is due within the first 60 days of the year, and the final declaration and payment at the end of February of the following year. The tax administration sets the instalments from your last tax amount.

## In summary

|                | Effective method                                           | Net tax rate                                                |
| -------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------- |
| What you pay   | VAT on sales minus input tax                               | A fixed percentage of your turnover                         |
| Input tax      | Deducted, every voucher counts                             | Built into the rate                                         |
| Declarations   | Quarterly                                                  | Semi-annual                                                 |
| Who can use it | Everyone                                                   | Up to CHF 5.024 million turnover and CHF 108,000 tax a year |
| Worth it with  | Many purchases, big investments, like a shop or a workshop | Few purchases, high margin, like consulting or an agency    |

## Who has to register

You're liable for VAT once your turnover reaches CHF 100,000 a year, in Switzerland and abroad ([Art. 10 VAT Act](https://www.fedlex.admin.ch/eli/cc/2009/615/en#art_10)).

## With nullish

Set your [methods](/en/accounting/vat/#methods) per year, any combination of the four. On the net tax rate, add the rate you were granted. nullish then splits the year into [VAT periods](/en/accounting/vat/#vat-periods), quarters or half-years, and fills in the [VAT declaration](/en/accounting/vat/#vat-declaration) from your invoices and entries.

At the end of the year, compare your declarations with your accounts: errors are corrected at the latest in the period that includes the 180th day after year-end. The [year-end closing](/en/learn/year-end-closing/) page covers it.

## FAQ

### Which method is cheaper?

It depends on your input tax. Compute both with last year's figures, like in the example above.

### Can I switch methods?

Yes, but only at the start of a tax period, after minimum durations set by the tax administration. Check them before you change, and ask your accountant if a switch is worth it.

### Can I deduct input tax with the net tax rate?

No. The rate already accounts for it.

### Do my invoices change with the net tax rate?

No. You still invoice VAT at the normal rate. The net tax rate only changes what you pay to the tax administration.

### Can I combine the net tax rate and the annual settlement?

Yes. You then pay one instalment on 30 August and settle the rest at the end of February.

### When do I have to register for VAT?

Once your turnover reaches CHF 100,000 a year. Below that, you can still register voluntarily, for example to deduct input tax.

{% aside type="note" %}
This page summarizes the VAT Act and the tax administration's 2025 rules. It isn't tax advice: for your own case, check with your accountant or the tax administration.
{% /aside %}
